<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">United Nations General Assembly</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> General Assembly is the main deliberative body of the United Nations, in which every member state has one vote. First meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in January 1946, it debates international questions, approves the budget and admits members on the Security Council's recommendation. Its resolutions decided the future of former Italian colonies: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> was to become independent by 1952, and in 1950 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> was federated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly.jpg" alt="United Nations General Assembly" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patrick Gruban, cropped and downsampled by Pine · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UN_General_Assembly_hall.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first session was convened on 10 January 1946 in the Methodist Central Hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, with representatives of 51 nations. Until it moved to its permanent home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>, the Assembly met at the former New York City Pavilion of the 1939 New York World's Fair in Flushing, and on 29 November 1947 it voted there to adopt the United Nations Partition Plan for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. It moved to the permanent headquarters in New York at the start of its seventh regular session, on 14 October 1952. In December 1988 it held its 43rd session in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> in order to hear <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasser-arafat">Yasser Arafat</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Its powers and procedures are set out in Chapter IV of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-charter">United Nations Charter</a>. It is the only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> organ in which all member states have equal representation, each with one vote. Most questions are decided by a simple majority; important questions, such as recommendations on peace and security, budgetary matters and the admission of members, need a two-thirds majority. The Assembly approves the budget and decides how much each member state must pay. It elects the non-permanent members of the Security Council and, with the Council, judges of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a>, and it appoints the secretary-general on the Council's nomination. The main annual session begins in September and runs for about three months.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Apart from budgetary measures, Assembly resolutions are not binding on members. Under the Uniting for Peace resolution of November 1950 it may consider a threat to the peace at once and recommend collective measures if the Security Council fails to act because of the negative vote of a permanent member. Membership grew from 51 in 1945 to 193, more than two thirds of them developing countries. In the 1980s the Assembly became a forum for a North–South dialogue between industrialised and developing countries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “United Nations General Assembly” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1945 Australia became an active middle power: the foreign minister H.V. Evatt was President of the <b>United Nations General Assembly</b> in 1948–1949, and Australia helped create the Colombo Plan of 1950 and the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation forum in 1989.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>United Nations General Assembly</b> called for sanctions in 1962, South Africa was barred from the Olympic Games from 1964 and expelled from the Olympic movement in 1970, protests disrupted its rugby and cricket tours, and after Biko’s death the United Nations Security Council imposed a mandatory arms…</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1975 members in the <b>United Nations General Assembly</b>, with Arab countries and the support of the Soviet bloc, pushed through Resolution 3379, a non-binding measure that equated Zionism with South Africa’s apartheid as a form of racial discrimination; the same year the movement created the Non-Aligned News…</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>At midnight on 14 August 1947 the Constituent Assembly's first session as a sovereign body closed with it, and that year the Indian delegation gave a recording of it to the <b>United Nations General Assembly</b> in New York as the national anthem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/jana-gana-mana">Jana Gana Mana</a></li><li><blockquote>Delegates from 51 nations gathered at Methodist Central Hall, Westminster, for the first session of the <b>United Nations General Assembly</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-10">10 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He appealed for peace at the <b>United Nations General Assembly</b> and celebrated Mass at Yankee Stadium.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-4">4 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
